  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nIsolated showers and a few thunderstorms continue to move across \nnorthern Kansas behind a weak outflow boundary and in modest high-\nlevel isentropic upglide in westerly flow aloft under east to light \nsurface flow. This pattern changes somewhat later today and tonight \nwith more southwesterly upper flow and have kept the bulk of tonight\ndry. Thunderstorm chances look to return to at least central Kansas \nThursday afternoon and evening as surface low pressure forms in this \narea. Convergence may be enough to overcome CIN per several CAMs. \nCAPE and shear could support a few strong to severe storms with \nmainly a wind risk. An increasing low-level jet could support this \nactivity into the evening but better convergence should shift north \nduring the night. The surface boundary looks to stay in the area \nFriday into Saturday before shifting south of the area into Sunday. \nPrecipitation chances remain modest until the front moves through \nduring the late weekend with greater coverage likely immediately \nbehind it, but shear and instability could allow for a few more \nstrong to marginally severe storms Friday evening and Saturday \nevening. A more zonal flow regime sets up early next week with could \nsupport some isolated to scattered convection.\n\nAs for temperatures and apparent temperatures, today's outflow and \nisolated precipitation have taken temps down slightly on average, \nbut not much impact. Dewpoints are also similar though slightly \nlower on average. Northern areas may struggle to reach Heat Advisory \nlevels today if the precip lingers but am not confident enough to \ncancel it. For Thursday, temperatures could be some of the warmest \nof the recent stretch in deeper southwest flow but even using some \nof the higher dewpoint forecasts that have verified better recently, \nnorthern areas struggle to reach Advisory levels. Concerning heat \ncould last into Saturday but if the dewpoint trends hold and the \nfront or outflow is a bit faster, this may not pan out. Overall, \nhave left the Excessive Heat Warning and Heat Advisory as-is and let \nlater shifts monitor trends. Noticably-cooler conditions remain on \ntrack for Monday and Tuesday.",
